WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.529
As a teenager growing up in

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:04.049
Greenwich Village in the late 1960s,

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.909
Steven Heller improbably

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.109
became the art director of

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.119
pioneering alternative publications

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.369
such as the New York Free Press,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.199
Screw Magazine and

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.699
the East Village Other, before

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.409
eventually going on to work at the

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.209
New York Times and teaching at the

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.759
School of Visual Arts for decades.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.169
He chronicles his youthful

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.089
misadventures in Growing Up

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.009
Underground, a memoir of

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.959
countercultural New York.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.699
In November, Heller spoke at the

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.679
Reason Speakeasy, a monthly

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.239
unscripted conversation with

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.069
outspoken defenders of free thinking

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.929
and heterodoxy in an age of

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.749
cancel culture and thought police.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.279
He regaled the Manhattan audience

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.079
with tales of arrests on obscenity

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.269
charges, how design and esthetics

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.189
can supercharge the meaning of

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.109
words and pictures,

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.339
and why so many in the

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.409
counterculture adopted exactly

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.719
the same uniform of alienation

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.159
in the name of individualism.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.378
Well, ladies and gentlemen, I want

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.179
to thank you very much for coming

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.799
out to the most recent

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.230
Reason Speakeasy.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.059
Today we are talking with Steven

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.099
Heller, the legendary art

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.809
director and graphic designer and

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.339
author of the phenomenal new book

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.745
Growing Up Underground:

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.039
a Memoir of Counterculture New York.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.599
Steve started out in the late

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.489
sixties as a teenager

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.409
working at bleeding edge alternative

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.659
outlets like the New York Free Press

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.919
and Screw Magazine.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.809
Of course, before becoming the art

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.309
director at the New York Times op ed

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.989
page for a bit and for the book

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.509
review section for many, many years,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:43.829
a longtime faculty member at the

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.719
School of Visual Arts, Steve brings

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.099
an irreverent and uniquely

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.739
knowledgeable position, perspective

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.419
to discussions of how alternative

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.009
and establishment publications

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.629
influence social and cultural

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.409
change.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.059
A fierce defender of free speech and

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.229
the First Amendment.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.999
He didn't just witness the past half

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.919
century of boundary busting changes

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.869
to media, he

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.399
helped make it all jump off the page

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.019
and grab our attention.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.119
Steve Heller, thanks for talking

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.228
at The Reason Speakeasy.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.699
Thanks for inviting me.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.709
All right. So let's start with

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.779
the memoir, which

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.849
also I will be sending all of you

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.859
a discount code

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.629
where you if you order it from the

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.919
publisher, from Princeton

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.809
Architectural Press, you can

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:25.859
get 20% off.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.869
So that's real savings in

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.529
inflation.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.449
Lots of gratitude for me.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.649
Yes.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.360
But it's a fantastic

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.239
personal story, but it's also a

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.739
story about a particular time and

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.609
place. Can you.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.919
What's the kind of elevator

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.349
pitch for your memoir?

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.419
The elevator pitch is I had ten good

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:46.420
years

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:48.550
and

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.209
I kept telling stories about

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.699
them and at

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.649
a certain point, since

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:58.689
I've written or edited

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.249
200 books or so,

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.919
I thought it was time to

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.839
get my story on the page.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.759
And various people

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.619
were encouraging me to do it

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:12.620
and

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.439
I wanted to kind of

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.419
cluster the, the years

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.509
together, how it's possible

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:23.510
to

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.929
get somewhere without going to

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:27.930
college

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.429
and still be part of the

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.889
cultural scene

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.000
and.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.279
And I like to tell stories.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.929
So essentially that's it.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.519
There was no elevator pitch.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.379
There was just calling up the editor

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.509
and saying, I have a memoir that

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.759
connects me to graphic design.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.409
Yeah. And since they published

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.389
graphic design books, it

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.319
happened.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.239
So the ten good years

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.139
start.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.669
What what year did that start?

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.079
In 1966.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.399
I was 16.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.349
So set the stage

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.149
because you grew up in Manhattan.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.859
I grew up in a small town in

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.409
Manhattan, Stuyvesant Town.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:11.669
So what?

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.529
What was 1966, New York

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.549
like? I know there's a couple of

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.379
other people who were here

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.059
at the same time.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.018
Tell us.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.599
Those of us who don't remember.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.089
Well, 1966, you could

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.679
drive a bus through

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.889
the Washington Monument.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:29.890
The.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.489
Washington Square.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.879
The arch. Arch.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:34.880
Yeah.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.579
Which was, you know, a big

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.589
thing because if you were in

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:43.839
the path of a bus, they didn't stop.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.629
Dylan was just coming on the radio

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:51.909
with Positively Fourth Street,

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:52.910
and

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:55.970
there was a.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.289
A kind of anticipation of

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.609
something happening.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.649
The Vietnam War was heating up.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.519
There were groups that were

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.379
founded to

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.629
protest the war.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.819
Civil rights in New York

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.369
was actually beginning to

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.800
start happening as a movement.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.749
Hippie culture was gradually

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.999
taking over the East Village,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.409
or what we used to call the Lower

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.410
East Side.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:32.159
And music

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.409
was making a major

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.410
splash

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.819
Fillmore East, The Anderson Theater,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.079
The Electric Circus,

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.109
the New York Academy of Music, where

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.969
I co-produced

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.309
with one of my the newspapers

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.919
I was with all these shows

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:50.920
at a 2,500

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:53.590
seat theater.

